WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:08.540
Andrew Wyeth: "I worked and worked and worked on just the field, that swell, that curve to the field."

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.420
"It's like building a house, and then living in it."

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.020
"I built the ground."

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:22.380
"Thinking of her... and then when I put it, it was right."

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:28.020
"That pink, like faded lobster shells that you find on the shore there."

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:38.020
Andrew Wyeth was 31 when he made "Christina's World" in Cushing, Maine, and it's become one of the most

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.820
recognizable American paintings of the 20th century.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:48.320
There is, unquestionably, a great power in it, and it's a power that is more accessible to the average person

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.580
than some of the prevailing experimentation of that period. At least it is for me.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:01:00.240
And that doesn't mean that it's any better or worse than a painting by say, Jackson Pollock from the same year.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:10.260
only that being realistic and dramatic, "Christina's World" more easily fits the shape of our memories, our dreams, our fears and cravings.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.040
In other words, it resembles a story.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.260
Okay. So what's the story Andrew Wyeth wants to tell with this painting?

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:27.100
What we can see is a woman lying in a field, looking up a gently sloping hill to a house and barn in the distance.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.040
The composition is elegantly designed.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:36.440
The first thing you notice is the woman. Her shape and her gaze draw you diagonally across the canvas

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.280
to the home in the upper right. From there, your eyes slip down the hill top to the barn,

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:48.120
then to the boundary where the tall grass meets the short, which curves forward and back down,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.040
dropping you off at the woman again.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:56.160
This constant movement from foreground to background intensifies the three dimensionality of the painting

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.940
already felt in the careful work Wyeth puts into the swell and curve of the field.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.000
Beyond this, there is a perfect asymmetrical balance

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.300
between the woman and the shapes in the top third of the canvas.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:12.880
These are design features that communicate a feeling of serenity immediately

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.500
without us even knowing why at first, like a bass note that's too low to hear,

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.740
but influences our experience nonetheless.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:26.520
And yet, as we begin to look closer at "Christina's World," a few things start to work against this column.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:33.420
For example, the colors, as in many of Wyeth's paintings, are muted, reflecting the particular austerity of

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.740
New England landscapes, a common image in early American art and thought.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:42.800
Even the woman's dress, which is the most striking color on the canvas, is a subdued pink.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.860
"Like faded lobster shells," as Wyeth describes it. The next thing that stands out is the woman's posture.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:55.060
Especially her thin and contorted arms. The way her hands grip the earth with a kind of nervous energy.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.940
They are the only hint of what might be going on in her head.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:05.000
You know, facing her away from the viewer, creates an absence that, in our interpretation of the scene

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.680
of the story, we're desperate to fill. So we put ourselves in her place.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:16.640
But that doesn't quite dispel the feeling of inscrutability, of not knowing that charges the painting with tension.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.260
There is more we can know about the woman in Christina's world if we want to.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.920
Christina is, after all, a real person.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.620
Anna Christina Olsen, who lived in the house from the painting with her brother, Alvaro.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.300
Olsen suffered from Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease,

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:37.140
a neuromuscular disorder that robbed her of the use of her legs. She refused to use a wheelchair however,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.380
preferring to crawl using the strength of her arms.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:48.340
"I saw her crawling out to a little, uh, truck guard that she had next to the house one day and uh, it dawned on me."

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:55.660
"And I went home that night. I remember there was a small party at... being given at the James's house"

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:04.820
"and I remember in the middle of party I rushed out into a room in the studio that I had painted in and kept my material"

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:11.800
"and made a quick notation of society of Christine. The field. The house in the background."

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:18.460
Olsen was 55 at the time of the painting, but if the figure and that pink dress looks more youthful,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:23.000
that's because Wyeth used his wife Betsy for a model of Christina's torso.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.060
Only the arms betray her real condition.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:31.380
To Wyeth, Christina's world wasn't the portrait of someone at just one point in time.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:37.600
It was a painting of Christina's whole life, her weakness but also her strength and vitality and, above all,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.580
the deep lifelong connection she had to this old family house in Maine and the land surrounding it.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.920
You know, by painting a symbolic representation of that connection,

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.260
by setting it in perpetual motion with his composition,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:54.920
by laboring for weeks and weeks on the grass and the shape of the field,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:59.480
Wyeth, I think was looking for a secret in nature.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:03.400
His work descends from the tradition of Emerson and Thoreau,

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:08.780
who found in the natural world the basis for a new American philosophy.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:15.340
Emerson writes in his essay, Nature, "Crossing a bare common in snow puddles at twilight..."

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:20.480
"under a clouded sky, without having in my thoughts any occurrence of special good fortune,"

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.320
"I have enjoyed a perfect exhilaration. I am glad to the brink of fear."

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:32.060
"I become a transparent eyeball; I am nothing; I see all."

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:39.660
"I often feel if I could only be... not there to paint,"

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.200
"just a pair of eyes, would be so marvelous."

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:51.380
"If you could just, I could just do something and disappear and just... it'd be terrific."

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:56.160
In his painting, Andrew Wyeth becomes Emerson's transparent eyeball.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:03.240
He sees in the nature around him, even in the barren landscapes of New England, something profoundly real.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:09.200
As an artist, he helps us to see it too, and it's a reminder, frankly, I need.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.200
Living in a city. Living online.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.320
It's easy to go about your day and see nothing at all.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.100
Now getting back in touch with nature isn't a promise of happiness,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.280
as the tensions in Christina's world are careful to show.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:28.260
But there is, I think, a piece of us in the land, in the trees, in the sky,

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:33.100
and a sense of wholeness waits for us when we can remember not to forget it.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.720
"I was there at the right moment."

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.260
"Imagine if I hadn't been there."

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:44.120
"But you see how important it is to be in the surrounding and breathe it?"

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:49.500
"And then it happens. If you're lucky, and you're perceptive enough to catch it."

